A **UAF** in V8 occurs when a pointer to an object is used after it has been freed, leading to memory corruption. A common vector involves weak references in **WebAssembly.Memory** or **ArrayBuffer** objects. An attacker can trigger UAF via **JSValue coercion** in an **Array.prototype.concat** operation after the buffer is deallocated but still referenced:

```javascript
const ab = new ArrayBuffer(0x100);
const dv = new DataView(ab);
// Trigger garbage collection (v8::internal::Heap::CollectGarbage)
gc();
// UAF: dv is now a dangling pointer
// Reallocate ab with attacker-controlled data
const evil = new ArrayBuffer(0x100);
const evilDv = new DataView(evil);
// Corrupt vtable or object structure
```

Exploitation extends via **type confusion** when **SharedArrayBuffer** (SAB) is involved. Attackers exploit **Spectre** via **Branch Target Injection (BTI)** to leak memory layouts and manipulate JIT-compiled code pointers. To bypass **Site Isolation**, the attacker exploits **cross-origin memory leaks** via **performance.now()** or **SharedArrayBuffer** timing channels (CVE-2020-6514). Mitigation involves **v8::internal::TaggedPointer** canaries, **W^X** memory policies, and **Control-Flow Integrity (CFI)** in JIT. Real-world exploitation was seen in **CVE-2019-13720**, exploited in the wild by **North Korean APT37**.

